  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 209 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nRidging over the Southern Plains will maintain hot and generally dry \nconditions across the region through Thursday morning. Afternoon \nhighs will range from the upper 90s along the Coastal Bend to around \n100-105 degF inland. When combined with dewpoints in the 60s and \n70s, heat index values are expected to peak between 108-114 degF, \nresulting in moderate to major Heat Risk (levels 2/3 to 4). A Heat \nAdvisory remains in effect for much of the area until 8 PM today. \nWhile conditions at quick glance look favorable for another advisory \ntomorrow, confidence is lower due to arriving rainfall associated \nwith Tropical Storm Bertha (more on this below!) sometime tomorrow \nafternoon. At this time we have held out on issuance and will \nreevaluate conditions with our overnight forecast package.\n\nSpeaking of Tropical Storm Bertha, satellite images indicate that\nthe center of Tropical Storm Bertha has made landfall near the \nBiloxi Wildlife Management Area in St. Bernard Parish, Louisiana. \nThe system continues to move slowly west- northwestward across \nthe northern Gulf. Satellite, radar, and NOAA and Air Force \nreconnaissance data indicates that persistent northeasterly wind \nshear is displacing the deepest convection south of the exposed \nlow-level circulation. Continued shear, dry air entrainment, and \nincreasing land interaction are expected to promote weakening over\nthe next 24-48 hours as the system tracks near the Louisiana \ncoast before moving inland over East Texas. While there remains \nsome uncertainty regarding the potential for a new circulation \ncenter to redevelop beneath the aforementioned convection, the \ncurrent forecast continues to favor the storm weakening and \ndissipating after moving inland.\n\nAlthough the center of Bertha is forecast to remain to our north, \nmodel data hints that its asymmetric structure will allow for \ntropical moisture and broad-scale ascent to extend farther south and \nwest than the circulation itself Thursday/Thursday night. This will \nallow for an increase in rain and storm chances. Rainfall coverage \nand amounts remain a little uncertain given Bertha's evolving \nstructure, but current guidance suggests generally 1-3 inches with \nlocally higher amounts beneath any heavier cells. Marine and coastal \nimpacts will also become more noticeable toward the end of the week \ndespite Bertha remaining north of the region. Long-period swell is \nexpected to build, increasing the risk for rip currents and \ndangerous surf over the next couple of days.",
